  <main>
    <article class="post wrap">
      <p>For 25 years, SEO meant one thing: rank in Google’s ten blue links. That world is ending. When someone asks ChatGPT, Perplexity or Google’s AI Overviews a question, they don’t get ten links — they get <strong>one synthesized answer</strong>, sometimes with a handful of citations. <strong>Answer Engine Optimization (AEO)</strong> is the practice of making your content the source that answer is built from and cites.</p>

      <h2>Why AEO is different from SEO</h2>
      <p>Classic SEO optimizes for a ranking algorithm that returns a list. AEO optimizes for a language model that <em>extracts and synthesizes</em>. That changes what matters:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Extractability</b> — the answer must be in the raw HTML, in clean, quotable sentences. AI crawlers don’t run JavaScript, so JS-injected content is invisible to them.</li>
        <li><b>Entities</b> — concrete, consistently-named people, products and places beat vague pronouns.</li>
        <li><b>Structure</b> — headings phrased as questions, FAQ blocks, tables and schema make content easy to lift.</li>
        <li><b>Citations &amp; trust</b> — named sources, statistics and author credentials make a model comfortable citing you.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The three gates every page must pass</h2>
      <p>Before AEO tactics even matter, a page has to clear three gates:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><b>Can the crawler reach it?</b> robots.txt and your CDN/WAF must allow GPTBot, PerplexityBot, ClaudeBot and Googlebot.</li>
        <li><b>Can it read it?</b> The content must be in the server HTML — not rendered by JavaScript after load.</li>
        <li><b>Can it trust it?</b> Clear entities, sources and structure so the model is confident quoting you.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>CiteRank’s <a href="/">LLM Access Check</a> tests the first two in seconds — fetching your page as each AI crawler and showing exactly what they can and can’t read.</p>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <p>Run any important page through the access check, fix anything that’s JavaScript-hidden or crawler-blocked, then run the <a href="/audit">full AEO audit</a> for a weighted score and ranked fixes. Do that across your top pages and you’ll be ahead of ~95% of sites, most of which haven’t thought about AI search at all.</p>

